{"answer":"AMD's 10-K describes Intel as its main rival across all three segments. In Data Center, AMD competes primarily against Intel and Nvidia, and also against Altera in FPGA and adaptive SoC server products. In Client and Gaming, 'Our primary competitor in the supply of CPUs and APUs is Intel'. For discrete graphics, AMD names Nvidia as the principal competitor and market share leader, with Intel making integrated graphics and gaming-focused discrete GPUs, and AMD says it is the market share leader in semi-custom game console products. In Embedded, 'Intel is our main competitor for embedded CPUs'. AMD does not say it is ahead of Intel in CPUs. It says only that it has a 'leadership roadmap' and 'leadership' products.

Intel's 10-K names AMD as its primary competitor in client and a competitor across the data center. For CCG, 'CCG's primary competitor is AMD, which like us designs processors based on the x86 architecture', and Intel also cites significant competition from ARM-based designers such as Apple, Qualcomm and MediaTek. For DCAI, competitors include AMD, 'which utilizes the x86 architecture and competes with us across the full spectrum of CPUs, GPUs, accelerators and other products'. Intel also lists NVIDIA, whose GPU systems have seen the highest demand, hyperscalers building custom silicon, ARM- and RISC-V-based entrants, and Broadcom in custom ASICs. Intel says the environment is highly competitive and expects it to intensify in 2026, and that data center CPU competition 'remained intense'. Its competitive framing is more defensive than AMD's.

In short, both name each other as the key x86 rival, but AMD frames the contest mainly around product leadership and AI growth, while Intel stresses intense, intensifying competition from AMD and others. Neither filing gives market share figures for the AMD-Intel contest.","figures":[],"declined":false,"missing_info":null}
